    "evidence_text": "Weight loss in cancer cachexia is attributable to decreased food intake and/or enhanced energy expenditure.\n\nUCP2 and -3 expression in BAT did not differ significantly between groups.\n\nThe Bile Acid Chenodeoxycholic Acid Increases Human Brown Adipose Tissue Activity.\n\nHere we examined the effects of oral supplementation of the bile acid chenodeoxycholic acid (CDCA) on human BAT activity.\n\nTreatment of 12 healthy female subjects with CDCA for 2 days resulted in increased BAT activity.\n\nWhole-body energy expenditure was also increased upon CDCA treatment.\n\nThese findings identify bile acids as a target to activate BAT in humans.\n\nTherefore, the aim of this study was to investigate the regulation mechanism of acetic acid on the hepatic lipid metabolism in dairy cows.\n\nAcetic acid consumed a large amount of ATP, resulting in an increase in AMPK\u03b1 phosphorylation.\n\nIn addition, activated AMPK\u03b1 inhibited the activity of acetyl-CoA carboxylase.\n\nConsequently, the triglyceride content in the acetate-treated hepatocytes was significantly decreased.\n\nInhibition of apical sodium-dependent bile acid transporter as a novel treatment for diabetes.\n\nBile acids are recognized as metabolic modulators.\n\nIn patients with cancer, the wasting syndrome, cachexia, is associated with caloric deficiency.\n\nHere, we describe tumor-induced alterations of the host metabolic response to caloric deficiency that cause intratumoral immune suppression.\n\nWhen these mice are challenged with caloric deficiency, the resulting relative hypoketonemia triggers a marked rise in glucocorticoid levels.\n\nMultiple intratumoral immune pathways are suppressed by this hormonal stress response.\n\nMetabolic effects of isoenergetic nutrient exchange over 24 hours in relation to obesity in women.\n\nThree groups of women were studied: lean, obese and 'post-obese' after slimming.\n\nEnergy expenditure was greater in absolute terms in the obese women.\n\nTwenty-four hour energy expenditure was lower by only 3-7 per cent when fasting compared to that when fed to achieve energy balance.\n\nSleeping energy expenditure was particularly low in the post-obese group when high-fat fed.\n\nIncreased resting energy expenditure and weight loss are related to a systemic inflammatory response in lung cancer patients.\n\nMATERIALS AND METHODS REE was measured by indirect calorimetry using a ventilated hood system.\n\nRESULTS Eighty-seven patients with primary non-small-cell lung cancer were consecutively entered onto the study.\n\n12%; 67 patients were considered hypermetabolic.\n\nTwenty-six patients had a substantial loss of more than 10% of their preillness weight.\n\nA switch from white to brown fat increases energy expenditure in cancer-associated cachexia.\n\nLimited therapeutic options are available and the underlying mechanisms are poorly defined.\n\nImportantly, UCP1 staining is observed in WAT from CAC patients.\n\nThus, inhibition of WAT browning represents a promising approach to ameliorate cachexia in cancer patients.\n\nComposition of gall bladder stones associated with octreotide: response to oral ursodeoxycholic acid.\n\nGall bladder bile was obtained by ultrasound guided, fine needle puncture from six patients.\n\nBile acids: natural ligands for an orphan nuclear receptor.\n\nBile acids regulate the transcription of genes that control cholesterol homeostasis through molecular mechanisms that are poorly understood.\n\nThese results provide evidence for a nuclear bile acid signaling pathway that may regulate cholesterol homeostasis.",
